SUMMARY:Nuclear Industry Panel Night - OPTIONS Program
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, November 19 | 5:00 – 7:00 PM | GB202 (Galbraith Building\, 35 St. George St.\, Room 202) | Register in Advance* \n*PLEASE NOTE THAT THIS EVENT IS AVAILABLE EXCLUSIVELY TO UOFT ENGINEERING GRADUATE STUDENTS. \nExplore the cutting edge of nuclear innovation and the need for talent in this rapidly evolving field. This special evening brings together industry leaders and academic voices to spotlight opportunities\, challenges\, and the future of the nuclear workforce development.  \nISTEP is proud to bring you this event with the support of our departmental co-sponsors: \n\nDepartment of Chemical Engineering & Applied Chemistry\nDepartment of Civil & Mineral Engineering\nThe Edward S. Rogers Sr. Department of Electrical & Computer Engineering\nDepartment of Mechanical & Industrial Engineering\n\nFeatured Speakers:  \nSean Granville\, Board of Directors\, NWMO | Former COO and Chief Nuclear Officer\, OPG | President of BNKA Consulting Inc. \nAs former Chief Operations Officer and Chief Nuclear Officer of Ontario Power Generation (OPG)\, Sean Granville was responsible for all of OPG’s generating fleet\, with a capacity of over 15\,400 megawatts of electricity. This consisted of 10 nuclear units\, 66 hydro stations\, two thermal stations\, one solar facility\, as well as oversight of OPG subsidiaries Atura Power and Eagle Creek Renewable Energy. Mr. Granville’s nuclear career spans 39 years including three years at Point Lepreau Nuclear Generating Station in New Brunswick. Mr. Granville’s energy industry experience includes a World Association of Nuclear Operators secondment\, as well as membership in the Canadian Nuclear Association and the CANDU Owners Group. Mr. Granville is also a board member of Women in Nuclear and past chair of the Canadian Chief Nuclear Officer Forum\, as well as a past member of the Institute of Nuclear Power Operations Executive Advisory Group. Mr. Granville is currently providing executive consulting services to the energy industry. Mr. Granville is a graduate of the University of Waterloo with a Bachelor of Applied Science in Mechanical Engineering.  \nLaurie Swami\, President and CEO of Nuclear Waste Management Organization  \nMs. Laurie Swami is the President and CEO of the Nuclear Waste Management Organization (NWMO). She was appointed to the role in 2016 and is responsible for implementing Canada’s plan for the long- term management of used nuclear fuel.   \nPrior to joining the NWMO\, Ms. Swami spent 30 years\, primarily in nuclear operations\, at Ontario Power Generation. She served as Senior Vice-President of Decommissioning and Nuclear Waste Management\, overseeing the operation of nuclear waste management facilities and planning for the decommissioning of a nuclear generating facility.   \nMs. Swami has extensive experience leading Environmental Assessment projects\, including nuclear refurbishments. She has appeared before Joint Panel Reviews (CEA and CNSC) for the Darlington New Nuclear Project and OPG’s Deep Geologic Repository Project. She holds a Bachelor of Science in Engineering Chemistry from Queen’s University and a Master of Business Administration from the Schulich School of Business. She is an ardent supporter of and advocate for the advancement of women to leadership positions. In recognition of this contribution\, she received the Women in Nuclear Canada Leadership Award in 2016.  \nPeter Keech\, Associate Professor\, Department of Chemical Engineering & Applied Chemistry  \nPeter Keech is an Associate Professor\, Nuclear Engineering within the Department of Chemical Engineering and Applied Chemistry. For 15 years\, he worked at the Nuclear Waste Management Organization where he led research programs around the world to advance the understanding of the container and sealing system that will be used in the deep geological repository for used nuclear fuel in Canada.    \nThrough work with universities\, national laboratories and international partner organizations\, he has over one hundred and twenty publications\, including more than ninety peer-reviewed journal articles and three book chapters related to chemistry\, nuclear waste\, corrosion\, microbiology and clay.    \nDr. Keech regularly presents both technical and non-technical information directly to scientists\, non-scientists\, partners and other stakeholders in the nuclear waste program. He provides support and technical review for several organizations\, including Nagra of Switzerland\, Posiva Oy of Finland\, SKB of Sweden and NWS of the United Kingdom and herecently served as a member of the Technical Advisory Committee for NUMO of Japan.    \nWhile at the NWMO and on behalf of its partner academic research community\, Dr. Keech co-chaired the committee on Indigenous Knowledge and Western Science within NWMO’s Strategic Initiatives group. He has organized and co-chaired workshops for a wide range of participants\, including professors and graduate students\, and Indigenous Youth and Elders\, to broaden perspectives and meaningfully contribute to Reconciliation. Dr. Keech was born in Orillia\, Canada.   \nChristina Dimitrov\, Senior Manager of Strategic Initiatives at OPG  \nChristina is a seasoned leader with over two decades of experience at Ontario Power Generation (OPG)\, where she has held diverse roles across business development\, enterprise strategy\, treasury and risk management. Currently Christina is a Senior Manager supporting New Growth at OPG\, pursuing strategic partnerships in support of the company’s growth portfolio. Christina’s career highlights include contributing to OPG’s corporate strategy development\, driving business optimization initiatives\, and financing large-scale hydroelectric projects.   \nChristina has achieved certifications in risk management\, treasury\, and project management\, complemented by an Honours Bachelor of Commerce. She is a supportive mentor\, and advocate for the advancement of women in nuclear and across the energy transition more broadly.  \nModerated by:  Maggie von Vaight\, Lecturer\, Institute for Studies in Transdisciplinary Engineering Education & Practice   \nMaggie von Vaight is a nuclear professional supporting the $13-billion Major Component Replacement (MCR) Program at Bruce Power\, one of Canada’s largest clean energy infrastructure investments. The program involves the refurbishment of six nuclear units and is helping secure Ontario’s supply of reliable\, carbon-free electricity.  \nWith more than a decade of experience in the nuclear industry\, Maggie has established expertise in procedural governance\, project controls\, and organizational learning\, driving performance and continuous improvement across complex\, highly regulated environments. She has served as a lecturer with the University of Toronto\, Faculty of Engineering and as a guest lecturer at the University of Guelph\, Lang School of Business where her teaching and industry engagement have focused on innovation\, commercialization\, and entrepreneurship\, connecting emerging ideas with industry application.  \nAn alumna of both the University of Toronto and the University of Guelph\, Maggie is a passionate advocate for education\, workforce development\, and collaboration between academia and industry. Through her leadership and mentorship\, she champions opportunities for young professionals\, fostering meaningful industry partnerships\, and developing the next generation of leaders who will build a stronger\, more innovative\, and sustainable future.   \nRegister in advance
URL:https://www.engineering.utoronto.ca/event/nuclear-industry-panel-night-options-program/
CATEGORIES:Info sessions,Lectures, seminars and workshops
END:VEVENT
